clearcote: opt-in HLSL shader dialect for a Windows persona on a non-D3D host.

WEBGL_debug_shaders.getTranslatedShaderSource() returns whatever the ACTIVE backend produced. A
Windows persona advertises a Direct3D11 renderer, but on a Linux host the Vulkan backend answers
with a SPIR-V dump, so two adjacent values contradict each other. Measured on our own builds, same
persona and UA:

    renderer  ANGLE (Intel, Intel(R) UHD Graphics 770 (0xA780) Direct3D11 vs_5_0 ps_5_0, D3D11)
    windows   "// INITIAL HLSL BEGIN ... #pragma warning( disable: 3081 ..."    806 bytes
    linux     "Outputs: gl_Position ... Paste the following SPIR-V binary ..."  7969 bytes

No reference data and no population baseline is needed to read this: the renderer string names the
API and the dialect sits right next to it.

ANGLE's HLSL backend is a source-to-source translator -- it needs no D3D device, only
angle_enable_hlsl at build time (added to the Linux args.gn; it is already on for Windows). So the
shader is re-translated with SH_HLSL_4_1_OUTPUT for THIS QUERY ONLY, using the context's real
ShBuiltInResources and the same ShCompileOptions Shader::compile builds, then wrapped in the
envelope ShaderD3D reports ("// INITIAL HLSL BEGIN" ... "<TYPE> SHADER END"). Verified: the result
is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the genuine capture from our own Windows build, sha256 50ce5778f1c1abab, 806
bytes both.

Rendering is untouched -- the Vulkan backend still compiles and draws the real shader. A WebGL
gradient draw with a 64x64 readback hashes 730b0805 with the option off, set to a junk value, and
on.

The hook is getTranslatedSourceWithDebugInfo() and its length query, which is what
glGetTranslatedShaderSourceANGLE actually calls (Context.cpp -> mImplementation->getDebugInfo()).
The translatedSource getters are deliberately left stock: they are unreachable from JS and are used
for real work. Both the length query and the buffer read go through one helper, because if they
disagreed the caller's read would truncate. That helper returns BY VALUE -- ShaderImpl::getDebugInfo
returns by value, so returning a reference to it dangles; the first cut did exactly that and hung
the query even on the honest path.

OFF BY DEFAULT, enabled per launch with CLEARCOTE_SHADER_DIALECT=hlsl. An environment variable
rather than a command-line switch because this code runs in the GPU process, which does not receive
the fingerprint switches, and ANGLE builds standalone so it cannot use base::CommandLine. Any other
value leaves every path stock.

Two guards, both failing toward the honest value:
  * if the active backend ALREADY emits HLSL (a real D3D host) the re-translation is skipped, so the
    same source is a strict no-op in the Windows build even with the variable set;
  * any translation failure returns empty and the caller falls back to the backend's real debug
    info -- a failed spoof must never destroy the truthful answer.

Cached per shader; the translation is deterministic and the getter can be called repeatedly.

Off by default because it is not free: the second translation is a different code path from the one
that rendered, so a shader the Vulkan backend accepts but the HLSL translator rejects falls back to
SPIR-V and reintroduces the tell for that shader alone. This is for users who hit this specific
check, not a default.

